    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d795">

                <pb facs="0882" n="688" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv02/pg688"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Hungary)/7–1645:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 795<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Representative in Hungary</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Schoenfeld</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d795-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Budapest</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-16T18:00:00+02:00">July 16, 1945—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>291. My telegrams 281<anchor n="2" xml:id="d795-f2"/> and 286,<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d795-f3"/> July 13.</p>
                <p>Key tells me meeting of ACC is scheduled for tomorrow night when he expects to
                    seek further clarification of Voroshilov’s note regarding changes in ACC
                    procedure. Key does not intend to commit himself to acceptance of Soviet
                    proposal pending further clarification and possible instructions, He is
                    particularly desirous of securing statement from Voroshilov as to meaning of
                    third paragraph of first section of latter’s note July 11 which seems to make
                    directives of ACC on principal questions dependent on agreement with American
                    and British representatives as proposed in your 57, May 28.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d795-f4"/></p>
                <p>Sent Dept repeated to Moscow as No. 26 and London as No. 7.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schoenfeld</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
